    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The appeal of a cobblestone driveway is easy to see. It recalls old European streets and courtyards, and natural stone setts, the squared blocks most people mean when they say "cobbles", can be extremely hard-wearing. In the UK, granite setts are a familiar sight on older streets and period driveways; in the US the same kind of squared stone is often sold as "Belgian block". Because cobblestone is a premium material, mistakes in planning or laying do not just spoil the look: putting them right usually means lifting and relaying stone you have already paid for.</p>
<p>It is tempting to think of a driveway as a surface job, but a cobblestone installation is really a series of layers, from the ground underneath to the joints between the stones, and each one has to be right. If you are considering this kind of investment, knowing the most common mistakes is the first step towards a driveway that lasts.</p>

<h2 id="foundation">1. Skimping on the foundation</h2>
<p>The most expensive mistake is neglecting the part of the driveway you never see. A cobblestone surface is only as stable as the ground beneath it, and some contractors keep their quote down by using a shallower sub-base or not compacting it properly.</p>
<p>Where winters bring repeated freezing and thawing, a shallow or poorly compacted base causes trouble. Water gets into the ground under the driveway, freezes and expands, and pushes the stones upwards; this is frost heave. Without a well-built base of compacted crushed stone that drains and spreads the load, even good granite setts will shift, rock or sink into ruts. There is no single correct depth: your installer or an engineer should set it for your soil and how the driveway will be used, and clay or soft ground generally needs more. Fixing a failed foundation means lifting the stones, rebuilding the base and relaying everything, which costs far more than doing it properly the first time.</p>

<!--figure:broken-paving-stones-in-sand|Without a deep, compacted sub-base, stones settle, rock and break.-->

<h2 id="material">2. Choosing the wrong stone</h2>
<p>Not all stone is suitable for a driveway. A common error is choosing setts on price or colour alone without checking how they behave outdoors under load. Some softer sandstones and lower-grade limestones look lovely when new but can spall, with the surface flaking away as moisture and temperature changes work on it.</p>
<p>Shape matters too. Tumbled setts have softened, rounded edges for a rustic look but create wider joints that need more upkeep. Cropped (split) or sawn setts have straighter sides and allow tighter joints. Above all, ask the supplier to confirm the stone is suitable for vehicle traffic and thick enough for the heaviest vehicles likely to use the driveway, including vans and delivery lorries; stone that is too thin will crack. Dense stones such as granite and basalt are usually the safer long-term choice.</p>
<p>If you like the character of reclaimed setts, make sure they have been cleaned of old mortar, tar and oil, and sorted for size and thickness, or they will not bed or joint properly with new materials.</p>

<h2 id="drainage">3. Ignoring drainage</h2>
<p>Water is the main enemy of any hard surface. A driveway needs a gentle fall so that water runs away from the house and off the surface rather than sitting on it; your installer will set the fall to suit the site, sometimes with a slight crown or a cross-fall.</p>
<p>Where water pools on cobbles it works its way into the joints. In winter that feeds frost heave; the rest of the year, damp joints encourage moss and algae, which break down jointing and make the surface slippery. On sloping or awkward sites, a channel (trench) drain across the bottom of the slope or a French drain along an edge may be needed to stop water undermining the base.</p>
<p>Drainage is also a planning matter. In England, a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area, such as a lawn or soakaway, within the property. A cobblestone driveway can be designed to be permeable with the right base and jointing, so discuss this at the design stage. A new dropped kerb also needs council permission. In the US, some areas charge stormwater fees linked to hard, non-permeable surfaces, and local permits or HOA rules may apply. Whatever your location, check with your local authority before work starts.</p>

<h2 id="jointing">4. Getting the jointing wrong</h2>
<p>What fills the gaps between your setts affects both the look and how long the driveway lasts. Traditionally, setts were laid with sand joints. These are flexible, but the sand needs topping up from time to time, and open sand joints invite weeds and ants, which can disturb the bedding and loosen stones.</p>
<p>There are two broad alternatives, and choosing the wrong one for your base is a common mistake:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rigid mortar jointing</strong> suits a rigid (bound) construction designed for it. Laid on a flexible base, mortar joints tend to crack as the ground moves slightly with the seasons.</li>
<li><strong>Flexible or permeable jointing</strong>, such as polymeric sand or permeable resin jointing compounds, suits a flexible base. A poor-quality product, or one applied in the wrong conditions, may fail to set and wash out in the first heavy rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever you choose, follow the jointing product's instructions to the letter, especially on joint width, depth and weather conditions, and ask the stone supplier which jointing suits their setts. A good permeable jointing compound costs more at the outset but helps resist weeds and movement, which saves time and repairs later.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask your installer to write down the stone, the base build-up, the bedding and the jointing product used. If you ever need to lift and relay a section, matching the original materials becomes far simpler.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="edging">5. Leaving the edges unrestrained</h2>
<p>Edge restraints are the bookends of your driveway. Without them, the sideways push of vehicles turning and braking makes the setts creep outwards. Gaps open up across the driveway and, eventually, the outer rows break away.</p>
<p>Lightweight plastic edging, or simply relying on the surrounding soil, is rarely enough for a driveway. Setts usually need a firm restraint such as a concrete haunch: a wedge of concrete laid along the outside of the edge stones and below the finished soil line, so it is hidden by turf or planting. Skipping this to save labour tends to mean relaying the edges much sooner than you would expect.</p>

<h2 id="installation">6. Treating it as a DIY or general job</h2>
<p>A small garden path in setts can be a manageable project for a capable DIYer, but a driveway is a different matter. The stone is heavy, a lot of it has to be moved and set by hand, and the levels and laying pattern need real precision.</p>
<p>Hiring a general landscaper with little stone experience is another frequent mistake. Laying setts well is a skill. Pattern errors such as failing to stagger joints create continuous lines that become weak points. Stones set at uneven heights leave a surface that is a trip hazard and difficult to clear of snow. Look for an experienced paving or stone contractor who can give references and show you similar driveways they have laid, ideally a few years old so you can see how the work has aged. Ask for an itemised written quote that states the base depth and material, the bedding, the stone, the edge restraint, the jointing, drainage and waste removal, so you can compare quotes on the same specification.</p>
<p>Be realistic about timing too. Because setts are laid by hand, the job often takes noticeably longer than pouring concrete: weeks rather than days for a typical driveway, weather permitting, and longer for intricate patterns such as fans or arcs.</p>

<h2 id="maintenance">7. Neglecting or over-cleaning it</h2>
<p>Even the best-laid driveway will deteriorate if it is ignored. Cobblestones are very durable, but they are not maintenance-free, and some of the most expensive damage comes from cleaning and de-icing the wrong way.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>De-icers:</strong> rock salt and harsh de-icing products can attack some stones and erode mortar joints. Use a product your stone supplier or installer approves, avoid ammonium-based de-icers, and keep salt away from fresh joints and metal.</li>
<li><strong>Pressure washing:</strong> a jet held too close can blast out jointing and mark the face of the stone. If you use one, choose a low setting and a fan nozzle, keep a sensible distance, follow the stone supplier's guidance, and avoid newly filled joints altogether. A stiff brush and a cleaner suited to the stone is often enough.</li>
<li><strong>Weeds and moss:</strong> brush them out of the joints regularly and top up or repair jointing as soon as it starts to fail. If you use a weedkiller, follow the label and keep it away from drains, ponds and watercourses.</li>
<li><strong>Sealing:</strong> seal only if the stone supplier recommends it, using a product suited to that stone; the wrong sealer can darken, film or trap moisture.</li>
</ul>
<p>Dealing with a loose sett or a cracked joint promptly is far cheaper than waiting until whole areas have shifted.</p>

<!--figure:weed-between-stone-pavers|Keep joints topped up and weeds out, but avoid harsh pressure washing.-->

<h2 id="cost-over-time">8. Cost over time</h2>
<p>A cobblestone driveway usually costs more upfront than asphalt (tarmac), concrete or gravel, mainly because of the stone itself and the hand labour. In return, a well-laid one can be very long-lasting, and individual setts can be lifted and relaid rather than the whole surface replaced. How long it actually lasts depends on the installation, the ground, the vehicles using it and how it is looked after. Most of the mistakes above shorten that life considerably, which is why getting the base, drainage, edges and jointing right matters more than saving on any one of them.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I lay cobblestones over my existing concrete driveway?</h3>
<p>It is generally not recommended. Laying setts over old concrete raises the surface, which can cause problems at the garage threshold, doorsteps, paths and damp-proof course, and any cracks in the old slab tend to show through the setts and joints. Ask a contractor to assess the slab; removing it is often the better option.</p>
<h3>How do I stop weeds growing between the stones?</h3>
<p>A good-quality polymeric sand or resin jointing compound, applied as the manufacturer instructs, helps resist weeds better than plain sand. Regular brushing and prompt repair of damaged joints matter just as much.</p>
<h3>Can a cobblestone driveway be cleared of snow?</h3>
<p>Yes, if it has been laid with a flat, even surface. Use a plastic or rubber-edged shovel or plough blade rather than bare metal, and if you use a snow blower, raise its skids so the blade does not catch on the edges of the setts.</p>
<h3>How long does installation take?</h3>
<p>Because setts are laid by hand, it often takes noticeably longer than pouring concrete: weeks rather than days for a typical driveway, weather permitting. Complex patterns, sloping sites and drainage work all add time.</p>
<h3>Are cobblestones slippery when wet?</h3>
<p>Natural granite and basalt setts with a split, tumbled or textured finish usually give good grip. Polished or very smooth stone, and any stone covered in moss or algae, can become slippery, so choose a textured finish for a driveway and keep the surface clean.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most cobblestone driveway problems come down to a handful of decisions: the base, the stone, the drainage, the jointing, the edges and who lays it. Get those right and the driveway can look good for a very long time. If you are still weighing up materials, compare the <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">pros and cons of a paver driveway</a>, read our step-by-step guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-driveway">how to build a paver driveway</a>, or see the <a href="/blog/driveway-apron-mistakes">driveway apron mistakes</a> worth avoiding where your drive meets the road. For help designing the front garden around your new driveway, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
